{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 0, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering effort to remove penalties for personal drug ownership will conclude this weekend, shifting back to criminalizing small amounts of potent substances. Under the newly enacted rule, HB4002, individuals found with illegal drugs—such as fentanyl, heroin, or meth—will face either prosecution or a mandatory recovery pathway. This includes enrolling in behavioral health programs and a diversion initiative to bypass fines. Still marked as a misdemeanor, possession could lead to jail time of up to half a year, while enforcement will focus more aggressively on public drug use. Additional strict measures now apply to those selling drugs near public spaces like recreational areas.\n\nLocal authorities emphasize that counties may establish their own diversion programs, though participation remains optional. Supporters argue this flexibility allows regions to tailor solutions to their capacities, while critics warn it could result in inconsistent enforcement. Portland’s police chief highlighted rigid eligibility for deflection, excluding those with prior charges, outstanding warrants, violent histories, or unstable medical conditions. Officers will relay details to a county dispatch team to assess qualifications and arrange immediate contact with mobile health responders within 30 minutes—or risk incarceration if delays occur.\n\nThe updated framework encourages—but does not require—diversionary approaches to redirect users from legal consequences toward treatment. However, the selective criteria and resource-dependent implementation raise concerns about fairness across different areas. Police stress adhering to strict timelines, leaving little room for negotiation if support systems fail to respond promptly.\n\n*** P.P.S.: The reversal marks a notable s